FINDINGS
------------------------------------------------------------
FINDING A (process / tree integrity - for the lead, NOT a defect in the work)
  Between 15:36:21 and 15:41:25 today the shared branch worktree
  /home/darrell/bin/ai/budget2/.claude/worktrees/trends-chart-cap did NOT
  contain the worker's change: web/static/js/insights.js had been restored to
  master (blob 563bf12), `git status --porcelain` showed no `M`, and
  `git diff master --stat` was EMPTY. Cause is visible in the leftovers: a
  concurrent checker ran mutation testing IN the shared worktree
  (/tmp/mutant-insights.js, mtime 15:36:21, = the worker file with
  `if (colorArr) newColor.push(colorArr[i]);` replaced by `// mutated out`)
  and restored from git rather than from a backup copy. The tree was restored
  correctly at 15:41:25 and is now back at blob 41c3da6.
  Two consequences the lead should weigh:
   - checker-second's verify server (/tmp/budget2-whatif-verify-8243/
     budget2-server) was BUILT at 15:41:39 - just inside the window's edge; if
     its build predates the restore its rendered evidence is about master, not
     the branch. Its build should be hash-checked before its verdict is
     accepted (curl /static/js/insights.js | git hash-object --stdin must give
     41c3da6...).
   - the worktree's per-worktree reflog shows only the 15:21:37 `reset: moving
     to HEAD` from `git worktree add`; the 15:36 revert left no reflog entry,
     i.e. it was a path-scoped restore. CLAUDE.md's "checkers work in a cp -a
     copy" rule was not followed by that lane.
  My own work never wrote to the shared tree: everything ran in
  <scratch>/tc1-tests/tree, on port 8241 only, and the subject blob was
  reconstructed two independent ways (from the captured diff applied to
  master, and from the mutant with the one mutated line restored) which
  produced byte-identical files hashing to exactly 41c3da6 - the blob id the
  worker's own diff header records.

FINDING B (Acceptance 1, test strength)
  The 38 px-per-bar coefficient is NOT mutation-killed by the unit suite:
  changing 38 -> 40 leaves all 6 new tests green (mutation C above). The cause
  is the spec's own fixture choice - with 3 categories max(360, 3*38+120) and
  max(360, 3*40+120) are BOTH 360, and the "full set" case (5 categories) is
  360 as well, so no assertion in the file is ever outside the floor region.
  The written criterion says "height max(360, 3*38+120)" and the test asserts
  exactly that, so criterion 1 is met AS WRITTEN; the coefficient is instead
  pinned by Acceptance 3's rendered heights (41->1678, 33->1374, 38->1564).
  Suggested follow-up: one more case with >= 7 categories so the unit test
  guards the coefficient too.

FINDING C (worker's two claims - both verified true)
  - "renderChart mutates its argument": charts.js:178-181 does
    `data.data = (data.data || []).map(function(trace){ return {...trace,
    orientation:'h', x: trace.y, y: trace.x}; });` - it reassigns the caller's
    `.data` property, so passing trendsChartRaw straight through would swap
    x/y on the stored payload. Mutation B (removing the deep copy) is killed
    by two tests, so the guard is real.
  - "the themechange restyle sets scalar markers so it cannot resurrect bars":
    confirmed by reading insightChartMarkers() (scalars only) and the restyle
    call ({marker: markers}, no x/y), and empirically - theme flip while
    expanded kept 41/33 bars. NOTE: this same scalar-marker design is what
    makes the FAIL above possible in the opposite direction.
